{"bill":{"id":"119-hr-8289","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"8289","title":"BIS Licensing Efficiency Act of 2026","introducedDate":"2026-04-15","latestActionDate":"2026-04-22","latestActionText":"Ordered to be Reported by the Yeas and Nays: 44 - 0.","policyArea":"Foreign Trade and International Finance","subjects":["Congressional oversight","Government information and archives","Government studies and investigations","Licensing and registrations","Trade restrictions"],"sponsorBioguideId":"M001137","cosponsorBioguideIds":["I000056","K000400","S000344"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"BIS Licensing Efficiency Act of 2026 This bill establishes a timeline for the Department of Commerce's Bureau of Industry and Security (BIS) to make decisions on applications for export licenses and establishes certain reporting requirements. Specifically, the bill directs BIS to make a decision on whether to approve an application for an export license and notify the applicant of the decision within 90 days of receiving the application. If no licensing decision is made within 120 days of submission of the application, BIS must notify the applicant of the status of the application, the reason a decision has not been made, and request any additional information necessary to make a decision. BIS must submit quarterly reports to specified congressional committees on the processing of license applications and other requests for authorization for the export, reexport, release, and in-country transfer of controlled items. The bill also requires the Government Accountability Office to audit and report to specified congressional committees on the license review process of BIS.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/8289","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hr/BILLSTATUS-119hr8289.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:29:53.564Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hr-8289","plainSummary":"This bill requires the Bureau of Industry and Security to decide on export license applications within 90 days and to explain any delays beyond 120 days. It mandates quarterly reports to Congress on license processing and directs the Government Accountability Office to audit the bureau's review process. The measure aims to speed up and add transparency to the approval process for exporting controlled items.","industries":[{"industry":"tech","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Technology companies frequently apply for export licenses for semiconductors, software, and hardware subject to export controls, so faster processing directly affects their compliance costs and time-to-market."},{"industry":"defense","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Defense and aerospace manufacturers routinely seek export licenses for controlled military and dual-use items, making licensing timelines a direct operational concern."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Manufacturers of industrial equipment and dual-use goods often require export licenses, so streamlined processing would reduce regulatory delays and costs."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"ee57f7a2842717580e28b7fa","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:33:40.304Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would set up a timeline for the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security to make decisions on applications for export licenses and set up certain reporting requirements.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys imported goods, and people who make goods here.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in prices, and what is made where. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-hr-8289","bioguideId":"M001137","c